nodecode-hermes-band is a rewrite of RegeneratusLabs's RegeneratusLabs/hermes-band:

  https://github.com/RegeneratusLabs/hermes-band
  commit c9dec797d9bf29fd5e09be538952c7db9bace8c8
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Regeneratus Labs (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the four tools the plugin gave the model -- band_render,
band_validate_score, band_list_presets and band_doctor -- with the same
arguments; the score as data (bpm, key, mode, bars, progression of roman
numerals, and one part per player with instruments, roles, fingering and drum
patterns, registers, start bars, gains, voices, jitter, swing and a mix of pan,
level and send); the four presets (bed, band, pulse, funk) with their tempos,
keys, bars and progressions; the theory that voices each chord by moving least
from the one before it; the humanisation (section arc, per-voice drum offsets,
jitter, swing); the MIDI writer (format 1, one track per part); and the render
chain -- fluidsynth per part, the stems summed with per-part pan and level, a
synthesised room built from feedback delay lines, a fade, peak normalisation to
-3.2 dB, ffmpeg's EBU R128 loudness pass and the mp3.

Left out: the plugin's numpy DSP that could not be carried without a Python
runtime -- the per-instrument EQ profiles, the FFT convolution reverb built from
a noise impulse response, the tilt EQ and the tanh glue saturation -- replaced
by a simpler deterministic room and the controllers each MIDI track carries; the
`hermes-band` command line (doctor/demo/check) and the audio-measurement report,
which read the rendered wav and are an operator's tool rather than a model's; the
five-bot session runner and the rap piece under session/ and pieces/, which are
worked examples in the repository, not the plugin; and the plugin's own
plugin.yaml settings schema, which became this cell's hermes-band config section.
